{
  "id": "proj-steel-plant-stand",
  "slug": "steel-plant-stand",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Steel Plant Stand",
  "summary": "A three-tier stand from square tube. Short straight welds, ideal for practising bead control.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 4,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Cut the tube with the angle grinder and a cut-off wheel: four 36 in legs, six 24 in long rails and six 10 in short rails. Each tier is a 12 x 24 in rectangle with the short rails butted between the long ones.",
    "Deburr every cut. Grind back to bright metal 1 in from each joint with the flap disc, then wipe the joints with acetone. Flux-core welds through mill scale, but the bead is much easier to read on clean steel.",
    "Lay out each tier frame on a flat surface with magnetic squares at the corners. Tack all four corners, check that the diagonals match, and then weld each corner.",
    "Practise on offcuts first. 16 gauge burns through easily, so start at the low end of the welder's chart and keep the beads short, around 1/2 in to 1 in.",
    "Tack the legs to the bottom frame 4 in above the leg ends, check each leg is plumb, then tack the middle tier at 18 in and the top tier flush with the leg tops. Finish-weld, alternating corners so the heat does not pull the frame out of square.",
    "Knock off the flux slag with the wire brush and dress any sharp spots with the flap disc. Wipe the frame down again, prime it, and give it two coats of satin black. Press the plastic caps into the leg ends.",
    "Cut the cedar into six 24 in slats, two per tier. Drill 3/16 in holes up through both walls of the long rails and screw the slats on from underneath. Weld and grind only in a ventilated space with the helmet, gloves and long sleeves on."
  ],
